{"id":"n-20261007-cs8q0t","category":"travel","display_title":"Bikepacking Scotland's Cairngorms After the Wildfires","display_title_he":"טיול אופניים בקיירנגורמס שבסקוטלנד לאחר השריפות","short_summary":"A gravel bikepacking trip through Scotland's Cairngorms National Park reveals a resilient landscape recovering from a major summer wildfire, though local tourism still feels the impact.","english_level":"C1","words":465,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/travel/2026/oct/07/cairngorms-wildfire-autumn-bike-ride-scotland","title":"‘It’s hard to imagine the fires of July’: a bike ride through the Cairngorms in Scotland","published_at":"2026-10-07T06:00:35.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-07T06:00:35.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-07T23:18:41.950Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["In the Cairngorms National Park, the transition into autumn brings a quiet sense of relief after a summer marked by environmental crisis. On July 15, a massive wildfire erupted near Glenmore and Abernethy, eventually scorching more than 33 square kilometers of moorland and ancient forest. The disaster demanded a month-long emergency response involving over 500 firefighters and triggering widespread evacuations. Today, however, the physical scars of the blaze are surprisingly elusive to the casual observer. Because the vast majority of the burned terrain lies high on remote hillsides far from public roads, the sheer scale of the park—spanning 4,528 square kilometers—absorbs the damage, leaving much of the landscape seemingly untouched.","To explore this resilient terrain, a small group of cyclists recently embarked on a multi-day journey to test new gravel bikepacking routes. The excursion, organized by Legendary Rides and Bikepacking Scotland, began with a trip from Edinburgh to Aviemore aboard an electric Ember bus, a modern transit option that accommodates bicycles. While the original itinerary required adjustments due to ongoing path closures near Glenmore, the riders bypassed the restricted zones by charting a course toward Nethy Bridge, pedaling through birch and rowan trees.","The environmental impact of the fire extends beyond visible charcoal. Locally, the community rallied during the crisis, with residents like Graham Niven, owner of a self-catering business called Arbor Nethy, joining volunteer firefighting efforts coordinated by the RSPB. Although the immediate danger has passed, the economic repercussions persist. Both Niven and Polly Cameron, who operates The Dell of Abernethy, have noted a distinct decline in autumn bookings. Sensationalist media coverage of an \"apocalyptic fire\" created a false impression that the entire region had been devastated, whereas, in reality, less than ten percent of the ancient Caledonian forest was affected.","As the cyclists pedaled eastward along the Speyside Way, the dense pine forests gave way to open grouse moors, sheep pastures, and historic landmarks. The route wound past the Glenlivet Distillery and led to the village of Tomintoul. The subsequent day’s ride traversed 18th-century military roads, passing Corgarff, a striking 16th-century white fortress that once served as a government barracks to suppress Jacobite sympathizers and combat the illicit whisky trade.","The journey concluded with a ride through the Balmoral estate toward Loch Muick, followed by a demanding climb over Capel Mounth and a rocky descent into Glen Clova. Throughout the trip, the cyclists navigated a complex mosaic of land ownership, crossing private estates managed for deer stalking alongside protected nature reserves. In Scotland, the right to responsible access permits travelers to traverse these diverse landscapes freely, provided they leave no trace. For those seeking to support the recovering local economy, autumn offers an ideal window to visit, characterized by thinning crowds, the departure of biting midges, and the rich, golden hues of the changing season."],"facts":[{"fact":"A massive wildfire started near Glenmore and Abernethy on July 15, burning over 33 square kilometers of moorland and forest.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The fire required a month-long emergency response involving more than 500 firefighters and evacuations of homes and businesses.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Most of the burned area is high on the hills and far from roads, making it easy to miss in the 4,528-square-kilometer Cairngorms National Park.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Local businesses, such as Arbor Nethy and The Dell of Abernethy, experienced a dip in bookings due to the reputation of the 'apocalyptic fire,' even though less than 10% of the old forest was affected.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Ailsa Sheldon tested new bikepacking routes in the park with representatives from Legendary Rides and Bikepacking Scotland.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"Cairngorms National Park","lemma":"Cairngorms National Park","pos":"name","he":"הפארק הלאומי קיירנגורמס","name":true},{"text":"transition","lemma":"transition","pos":"noun","he":"מעבר","name":false},{"text":"autumn","lemma":"autumn","pos":"noun","he":"סתיו","name":false},{"text":"relief","lemma":"relief","pos":"noun","he":"הקלה","name":false},{"text":"environmental","lemma":"environmental","pos":"adjective","he":"סביבתי","name":false},{"text":"crisis","lemma":"crisis","pos":"noun","he":"משבר","name":false},{"text":"wildfire","lemma":"wildfire","pos":"noun","he":"שריפת יער","name":false},{"text":"erupted","lemma":"erupt","pos":"verb","he":"פרצה","name":false},{"text":"Glenmore","lemma":"Glenmore","pos":"name","he":"גלנמור","name":true},{"text":"Abernethy","lemma":"Abernethy","pos":"name","he":"אברנתי","name":true},{"text":"scorching","lemma":"scorch","pos":"verb","he":"חורכת, שורפת","name":false},{"text":"square kilometers","lemma":"square kilometer","pos":"noun","he":"קילומטרים רבועים",